@page "/components/fileinput"
@page "/components/file-input"

<PageOutlet Url="components/fileinput" Title="FileInput"
            Description="A file selection component with browse, drag-and-drop and clipboard paste support, built-in size, total size, extension, MIME type, count, duplicate, image resolution and custom validation, a built-in drop zone panel, image preview thumbnails and file type glyphs, folder and camera capture modes, described constraints and screen reader announcements, and a fully templatable file list - selection only, no uploading involved." />

<DemoPage Name="FileInput"
          Description="BitFileInput is the selection half of working with files: it lets the user hand files to the app - through the file dialog, by dragging them out of the OS onto the component, or by pasting them from the clipboard - without uploading anything anywhere. Each selected file surfaces in C# as a BitFileInputInfo carrying its name, size, MIME type, last-modified time, the folder it came from and, on request, the pixel dimensions of an image, and its content can be pulled on demand as a byte array. Selections are validated on arrival against per-file size bounds, a total size budget, allowed extensions or MIME types, a maximum file count, a no-duplicates rule and any custom rule; files that fail stay in the list, marked invalid with a customizable message, so the user can see exactly what went wrong, and the list level limits release their files again as soon as removals free up room. The built-in file list shows names, human-readable sizes, image thumbnails or file type glyphs and per-file remove buttons, and both the browse area and the list items can be replaced wholesale with templates. Accessibility is wired in rather than bolted on: the constraints can be spelled out in a description tied to the browse button through aria-describedby, and every change of the list is announced through an ARIA live region. When it is time to actually send files to a server, reach for the BitFileUpload component instead."
          Parameters="componentParameters" SubEnums="componentSubEnums" SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers" CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubUrl="Inputs/FileInput/BitFileInput.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Inputs/FileInput/BitFileInputDemo.razor">
    <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
        <div>
            Three ways in, all on by default: click the browse button to open the file dialog, drag a file out of
            the OS and drop it anywhere on the component, or focus it and paste a file from the clipboard. The
            selection lands in the built-in file list with its name and a human-readable size, and the browse button
            turns into a dashed drop indicator while files hover over the component. <b>Label</b> sets the button
            text (default "Browse") and <b>Title</b> its tooltip. Disabling the component blocks all three routes
            and takes the button out of the tab order.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" Title="Pick one file from your device" />

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Disabled file input" IsEnabled="false" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="AllowDrop & AllowPaste" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode"
                 Id="example2">
        <div>
            The two alternative routes switch off independently and take effect immediately.
            <b>AllowDrop</b> off still swallows the drop event, so the browser does not navigate away to the file
            and lose the page state, but shows a "no drop" cursor instead of handling it. <b>AllowPaste</b> off
            ignores pasted files; note that a paste only reaches the component while the focus is inside it, so it
            is a power-user shortcut rather than the primary route.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitCheckbox @bind-Value="allowDrop" Label="AllowDrop" />
        <br />
        <BitCheckbox @bind-Value="allowPaste" Label="AllowPaste" />

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse, drop or paste a file" AllowDrop="allowDrop" AllowPaste="allowPaste" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Description" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" Id="example3">
        <div>
            Telling the user the rules before they run into them is the biggest usability win a file input offers.
            <b>Description</b> renders a hint under the browse button <i>and</i> wires it to that button through
            aria-describedby, so a screen reader reads the constraints together with the button instead of leaving
            them to be discovered by trial and error. Spell out there whatever <b>Accept</b>,
            <b>AllowedExtensions</b> and <b>MaxSize</b> actually enforce. <b>DescriptionTemplate</b> takes over when
            the hint needs markup of its own.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a document" Accept=".pdf,.docx" MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 5"
                      Description="PDF or DOCX, up to 5 MB." />

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop an image" Accept="image/*" MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 2">
            <DescriptionTemplate>
                <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--Info" />
                <span>Square images look best. Up to <b>2 MB</b>.</span>
            </DescriptionTemplate>
        </BitFileInput>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Multiple & Directory" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" Id="example4">
        <div>
            <b>Multiple</b> lets the dialog hand over several files at once and lets a single drop or paste carry
            several. Without it the dialog is limited to one file and only the first file of a multi-file drop is
            taken, rather than the rest flooding the list.
            <br /><br />
            <b>Directory</b> goes further and switches the dialog to folder selection: every file inside the picked
            folder, subfolders included, lands in the list. It also teaches the drop zone to walk a dropped folder
            recursively, which a plain file drop cannot do since the browser hands over no files at all for a
            directory. Each file then carries its <b>RelativePath</b> inside that folder, and the list prints the
            folder next to the size - without it a flattened folder is a wall of names with no way to tell two
            <code>readme.md</code> files apart. All the validations still apply to each file individually.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple />

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a folder" Directory />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Removable" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" Id="example5">
        <div>
            <b>ShowRemoveButton</b> puts a remove button on each file item. Removing a file updates the list,
            raises <b>OnRemove</b> and <b>OnChange</b>, re-runs the list level limits and releases the file's
            reference on the JavaScript side so the browser can free the memory. Because the pressed button leaves
            the DOM with its file, the focus is handed on to the button that takes its place - to the last one when
            the list got shorter, and back to the browse button when nothing is left - so removing several files in
            a row from the keyboard never drops the focus back to the top of the page.
            <b>RemoveButtonIconName</b> swaps the default Delete icon for any other built-in icon.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowRemoveButton />

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowRemoveButton RemoveButtonIconName="Cancel" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Append, AutoReset & duplicates" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" Id="example6">
        <div>
            Three parameters decide how a new selection meets the one already in the list. By default it replaces
            it. <b>Append</b> keeps the existing files and adds the new ones to the end, letting the user build the
            list up over several rounds of browsing, dropping or pasting. <b>AutoReset</b> is the opposite
            extreme: it empties the list just before the dialog opens, so every browse starts clean even if the
            dialog is then cancelled.
            <br /><br />
            Building a list up makes it easy to pick the same file twice. <b>AllowDuplicates</b> set to false
            catches that: a file matching one already in the list by folder, name, size and last-modified time is
            marked invalid with the <b>DuplicateErrorMessage</b> rather than added as a second working entry. The
            duplicate stays visible so the user understands why nothing new appeared, and like every list level
            rule it is re-evaluated after each removal - dismissing either copy leaves the survivor valid. Select
            the same file twice below to see it.
        </div>

        <br />

        <div>Append with no duplicates:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple Append ShowRemoveButton AllowDuplicates="false"
                      DuplicateErrorMessage="This file has already been picked." />

        <br /><br />

        <div>AutoReset:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" AutoReset />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Size & count limits" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" Id="example7">
        <div>
            Four parameters bound what the selection may carry. <b>MaxSize</b> and <b>MinSize</b> bound each file
            individually, <b>MaxTotalSize</b> bounds the accumulated size of the whole list, and <b>MaxCount</b>
            caps how many files it holds. A file outside the bounds is not thrown away: it stays in the list marked
            invalid, with an error message of its own and a red rule around it, so the user sees exactly which file
            did not make it. Each message is customizable through the matching
            <b>...ErrorMessage</b> parameter.
            <br /><br />
            The two list level limits are re-run after every change, so a file rejected for the budget or the cap
            becomes valid again as soon as removals free up room, and only files that are otherwise valid consume a
            slot or a byte of the budget - a file rejected for its size never pushes a good file over the cap.
        </div>

        <br />

        <div>Per file, 1 MB max and 1 KB min, with a custom message:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 1" MinSize="1024"
                      MaxSizeErrorMessage="The file is too big! Please select a file smaller than 1 MB." />

        <br /><br />

        <div>Up to 3 files and 2 MB in total:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple Append ShowRemoveButton
                      MaxCount="3" MaxTotalSize="1024 * 1024 * 2" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Accept, AllowedExtensions & Capture" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" Id="example8">
        <div>
            Two parameters restrict file types at different points. <b>Accept</b> feeds the native accept
            attribute, so the file dialog only offers matching files - but a drop or a paste never goes through the
            dialog, which makes it no more than a convenience filter. <b>AllowedExtensions</b> is the actual guard:
            it checks every file however it arrived, marking mismatches invalid with the
            <b>NotAllowedExtensionErrorMessage</b>, and when <b>Accept</b> is not set it generates the accept
            attribute from the same list. Its entries are matched leniently - an extension with or without its
            leading dot, in any casing - and an entry containing a slash is treated as a MIME type instead, matched
            against the type the browser reports, with a trailing wildcard like <b>image/*</b> covering a whole
            group. That accepts every image regardless of its extension, which a plain extension list cannot
            express.
        </div>

        <br />

        <div>Accept (dialog filter only):</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse images" Accept="image/*" />

        <br /><br />

        <div>AllowedExtensions (validated):</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" AllowedExtensions="@([".gif", ".jpg", ".png", ".bmp"])" />

        <br /><br />

        <div>AllowedExtensions with MIME types (validated):</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" AllowedExtensions="@(["image/*", "application/pdf"])" />

        <br /><br />

        <div>
            <b>Capture</b> is the third native attribute of the picker: it asks a mobile device to open a camera
            or a microphone instead of the file browser - "user" for the front camera, "environment" for the rear
            one - and needs an <b>Accept</b> of its own to say which of the two. Desktop browsers ignore it and
            show the regular dialog.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Take a photo" Accept="image/*" Capture="environment" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Custom validation" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
        <div>
            <b>FileValidator</b> runs a function of your own for each newly selected file once the built-in checks
            pass. Returning a message marks the file invalid and shows it under the name; returning null accepts
            the file. Any rule expressible over the file's metadata works, and a validator that throws only
            invalidates its own file - using the exception message as the error text - rather than aborting the
            whole selection.
            <br /><br />
            Pixel dimensions are the one property of an image that neither its name nor its metadata reveals, yet
            avatars, banners and thumbnails are exactly what needs a resolution rule. <b>ReadImageDimensions</b>
            makes the component decode every selected image and fill <b>Width</b> and <b>Height</b> before
            validation runs, so the validator can reject on resolution and name the offending size. Decoding costs
            time and memory in proportion to the images, which is why it is opt-in; anything that is not a
            decodable image comes back with both properties null.
        </div>

        <br />

        <div>Rejecting empty files:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple FileValidator="@ValidateEmptyFile" />

        <br /><br />

        <div>Rejecting images below 300&times;300:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop images" Multiple Accept="image/*" ReadImageDimensions
                      FileValidator="@ValidateImageDimensions" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Preview & file icons" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example10CsharpCode"
                 Id="example10">
        <div>
            <b>ShowPreview</b> puts a thumbnail next to each image, backed by an object URL the component creates
            on selection and revokes when the file is removed or the component is disposed - no manual cleanup, and
            the URL is also exposed as <b>PreviewUrl</b> for a custom <b>FileViewTemplate</b>. Everything that has
            no thumbnail gets a glyph for its type in the same slot, picked from the MIME type and the extension,
            so the names of a mixed list stay in one column. <b>FileIconSelector</b> replaces that choice with one
            of your own, and returning null from it leaves a file with no glyph at all.
        </div>

        <br />

        <div>Images and documents together:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple ShowPreview />

        <br /><br />

        <div>A selector of your own:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple ShowPreview FileIconSelector="@SelectFileIcon" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Localization" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example11CsharpCode" Id="example11">
        <div>
            Every piece of English the component can say has a parameter that replaces it, and they are the whole
            list: <b>Label</b> and <b>Description</b> for the browse area, <b>RemoveButtonTitle</b> for the remove
            button's tooltip and the prefix of its accessible label ("Remove report.pdf"),
            <b>FileListAriaLabel</b> for the accessible name of the file list, the six
            <b>...ErrorMessage</b> parameters for the validation messages, <b>FileSizeFormatter</b> for the file
            size - which is also where a different rounding or the decimal base some platforms prefer belongs - and
            <b>AnnouncementProvider</b> for the sentence the screen reader hears on every change of the list.
            Setting them once for a whole app is what the <BitLink Href="#example19">cascading parameters</BitLink>
            are for.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="انتخاب یا رها کردن فایل" Multiple ShowRemoveButton
                      MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 1" RemoveButtonTitle="حذف"
                      MaxSizeErrorMessage="حجم فایل از حد مجاز بیشتر است."
                      FileSizeFormatter="@FormatFileSizeInFarsi"
                      AnnouncementProvider="@AnnounceInFarsi" />

        <br />

        <div>Raw byte counts instead of the built-in humanizer:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple FileSizeFormatter="@(size => $"{size:N0} bytes")" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="HideFileList" RazorCode="@example12RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example12CsharpCode" Id="example12">
        <div>
            <b>HideFileList</b> suppresses the built-in file list while the selection keeps working - for an app
            that renders its own view of the files elsewhere. The example captures the selection through
            <b>OnChange</b> and renders a plain list of its own below. Its sibling <b>HideLabel</b> does the same
            for the browse button, as the Public API section shows.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple HideFileList OnChange="@HandleOnHiddenListChange" />

        <br />

        <div>Custom file list:</div>
        <br />
        @if (hiddenListFiles.Length == 0)
        {
            <div>No files selected yet.</div>
        }
        @foreach (var file in hiddenListFiles)
        {
            <div>@file.Name - @FileSizeHumanizer.Humanize(file.Size)</div>
        }
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Events" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13">
        <div>
            <b>OnChange</b> fires with the current array of files whenever the selection changes - after browsing,
            dropping, pasting and removing alike. <b>OnInvalid</b> follows it whenever the list holds at least one
            invalid file, carrying only those files with their messages, which saves filtering the list by hand to
            drive a summary or block a submit button. <b>OnRemove</b> fires once per removed file. Since content is
            never loaded eagerly, <b>ReadContentAsync</b> fetches bytes on demand - pass a file to read just that
            one, or call it with no argument to read every valid file, which is what the example does before
            showing the loaded byte count next to each file along with its <b>LastModifiedDate</b>.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput @ref="eventsFileInput" Label="Select or drag and drop files" Multiple ShowRemoveButton
                      MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 1" OnChange="@HandleOnChange" OnInvalid="@HandleOnInvalid"
                      OnRemove="@HandleOnRemove" />

        <br />
        <br />

        <div>Selected files:</div>
        <br />
        @foreach (var file in eventsFiles)
        {
            <div>
                @file.Name (@FileSizeHumanizer.Humanize(file.Size), modified @file.LastModifiedDate.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd"))
                @if (file.Content is not null)
                {
                    <span> - @file.Content.Length bytes loaded</span>
                }
            </div>
        }

        @if (eventsLog.Count > 0)
        {
            <br />
            <div>Events:</div>
            <br />
            @foreach (var log in eventsLog)
            {
                <div class="event-log">@log</div>
            }
        }
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Templates" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
        <div>
            The default UI can be swapped out entirely: <b>LabelTemplate</b> replaces the browse button - here with
            a full drop-zone panel that hides itself once files are selected - and <b>FileViewTemplate</b> replaces
            each item of the file list, receiving the file info as its context, validation state and error message
            included. Replacing the browse button also replaces the built-in dashed drop indicator that lives on
            it, so a custom label should bring its own drag feedback: <b>Classes.Dragging</b> puts a class on the
            root element for as long as files hover over the component, which is what turns the panel below blue.
            For a drop panel that is not this hand-built, reach for
            <BitLink Href="#example18"><b>ShowDropZone</b></BitLink> instead - it brings the same shape with the
            focus ring, the drag state and the theme already wired in.
        </div>

        <br />

        <div>
            <BitFileInput @ref="bitFileInput" Multiple MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 2"
                          AllowedExtensions="@([".jpg", ".jpeg", ".png", ".bmp"])"
                          Classes="@(new() { Dragging = "custom-drop-zone" })">
                <LabelTemplate>
                    @if (bitFileInput.Files?.Any() is not true)
                    {
                        <div class="browse-file" @onclick="() => bitFileInput.Browse()">
                            <div class="browse-file-header">
                                <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--CloudUpload" />
                                <div>
                                    Drag and drop or
                                </div>
                                <div>
                                    <strong>
                                        Browse files
                                    </strong>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="browse-file-footer">
                                <div>Max file size: 2 MB</div>
                                <div>Supported file types: jpg, jpeg, png, bmp</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </LabelTemplate>
                <FileViewTemplate Context="file">
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(file.Name))
                    {
                        <div class="file-list">
                            <div class="file-list-header">
                                <div class="file-info">
                                    <div class="file-info-main">
                                        <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--Page" />
                                        <div class="file-info-data">
                                            <div class="file-info-name">
                                                @file.Name
                                            </div>
                                            <div>
                                                @FileSizeHumanizer.Humanize(file.Size)
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>

                                    <div class="file-info-btns">
                                        <i class="bit-icon bit-icon--Cancel remove-ico"
                                           @onclick="() => bitFileInput.RemoveFile(file)" />
                                    </div>
                                </div>

                                @if (!file.IsValid)
                                {
                                    <div class="file-info-e-msg">@file.Message</div>
                                }
                            </div>

                            <div class="file-list-footer">
                                <div>Max file size: 2 MB</div>
                                <div>Supported file types: jpg, jpeg, png, bmp</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }
                </FileViewTemplate>
            </BitFileInput>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Public API" RazorCode="@example15RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example15CsharpCode" Id="example15">
        <div>
            The component can be driven entirely from code: <b>Browse</b> opens the file dialog, <b>Reset</b>
            silently clears the selection without raising any callback, and <b>RemoveFile</b> removes a specific
            file - or every file when called without an argument - raising <b>OnRemove</b> and <b>OnChange</b> just
            like the remove button does. <b>Files</b> exposes the current selection as a read-only list, each entry
            carrying its position in <b>Index</b>, and <b>InputId</b> gives the id of the underlying HTML input.
            Here the browse button is hidden via <b>HideLabel</b> and replaced with external BitButtons - and
            since that button is also what the drop indicator lives on, hiding it moves the indicator onto the
            component itself, so dropping files onto the list still shows that it is allowed.
            <br /><br />
            <b>OpenReadStreamAsync</b> is the other way to get at a file's bytes: the runtime reads it from the
            browser in chunks instead of materializing it whole the way <b>ReadContentAsync</b> does, which is
            what makes a file too large to hold as a byte array - a video, an archive, a database dump - copyable
            to disk, hashable or forwardable to a server. It reads at most as many bytes as the browser reported
            for the file unless a different ceiling is passed, and the stream is the caller's to dispose.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput @ref="publicApiFileInput" HideLabel Multiple OnChange="@(_ => StateHasChanged())" />

        <br />

        <div class="api-buttons">
            <BitButton OnClick="() => publicApiFileInput.Browse()">Browse files</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="() => publicApiFileInput.Reset()">Reset</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="() => publicApiFileInput.RemoveFile()">Remove all</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="@HashTheFirstFile">Hash the first file</BitButton>
        </div>

        <br />

        <div>@(publicApiFileInput?.Files.Count ?? 0) file(s) in the list.</div>

        @if (streamHash.HasValue())
        {
            <br />
            <div>SHA-256: <code>@streamHash</code></div>
        }
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Accessibility" RazorCode="@example16RazorCode" Id="example16">
        <div>
            The browse button is a real button - reached with Tab, activated with Enter or Space, drawing the
            theme's focus ring, and removed from the tab order while the component is disabled - and the hidden
            native input points at it through aria-labelledby, so screen readers announce the visible label. The
            file list carries list and listitem roles and an accessible name of its own - "Selected files" unless
            <b>FileListAriaLabel</b> says otherwise - each remove button an aria-label naming the file it removes
            and the focus after that file is gone, and an invalid file is marked by a message, a red rule and,
            where the palette is stripped away in Windows High Contrast, a dashed rule. A file name is truncated
            without losing its extension. A visually hidden live region announces every change of the list,
            so a screen reader user hears how many files were taken and how many failed validation without going
            looking - and because a live region is skipped when its text repeats verbatim, each update carries an
            invisible marker so two identical selections in a row are both announced.
            <b>AnnouncementProvider</b> replaces that sentence (see <BitLink Href="#example11">Localization</BitLink>),
            and <b>AriaLabel</b> labels the browse button where a short visible label like "Browse" does not say
            enough on its own. Dragging is never the only way in either - everything a drop does, the browse
            button does with a single tap - and both it and the remove buttons stay at or above the 24 CSS pixel
            target size in every <b>Size</b>.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" AriaLabel="Select a document to attach"
                      Description="Tab to the button, then press Enter or Space." />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Variant" RazorCode="@example17RazorCode" Id="example17">
        <div>
            <b>Variant</b> decides how much of the <b>Color</b> the browse button carries: <b>Fill</b>, the default,
            paints the whole button, <b>Outline</b> keeps only the rule around it, and <b>Text</b> drops both so the
            button weighs no more than the text it holds - which is what you want when the file input sits among
            other form fields and should not read as the primary action of the page. All three fill in on hover and
            turn into the same dashed drop zone while a file is dragged over them.
        </div>

        <br />

        <div class="example-box">
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Fill" Label="Fill" />
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Label="Outline" />
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Text" Label="Text" />
        </div>

        <br /><br />

        <div><b>Disabled</b>:</div>
        <br />

        <div class="example-box">
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Fill" Label="Fill" IsEnabled="false" />
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Label="Outline" IsEnabled="false" />
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Text" Label="Text" IsEnabled="false" />
        </div>

        <br /><br />

        <div><b>Variant</b> & <b>Color</b>:</div>
        <br />

        <div class="example-box">
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Fill" Color="BitColor.Success" Label="Fill" />
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.Success" Label="Outline" />
            <BitFileInput Variant="BitVariant.Text" Color="BitColor.Success" Label="Text" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Drop zone" RazorCode="@example18RazorCode" Id="example18">
        <div>
            <b>ShowDropZone</b> turns the browse area into the panel a drag-and-drop target is expected to look
            like - a dashed rule around a glyph and the label - instead of a button that happens to accept drops.
            It is the same button underneath, so Tab still reaches it and Enter or Space still opens the dialog,
            and it fills with the theme color while files hover over it exactly as the button does. Because a
            panel-sized block of the role color is rarely what a drop zone wants, it defaults to the
            <b>Outline</b> variant; set <b>Variant</b> to take that back. <b>DropZoneIconName</b> swaps the
            default CloudUpload glyph - or drops it entirely when set to an empty string - and
            <b>DropZoneIcon</b> takes one from an external icon library.
        </div>

        <br />

        <BitFileInput ShowDropZone Multiple ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton
                      Label="Drag files here or click to browse"
                      Description="Any file type, as many as you like." />

        <br />

        <BitFileInput ShowDropZone Directory DropZoneIconName="FabricFolder"
                      Label="Drag a folder here or click to browse" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example19RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example19CsharpCode"
                 Id="example19">
        <div>
            <b>BitParams</b> carries a <b>BitFileInputParams</b> down to every file input under it, so a form, a
            wizard step or a whole app settles the shared rules once instead of repeating them on every input -
            which is what the size limits, the localized messages and the size formatter of a real app actually
            are. What it carries is a default and not an override: an input that writes a parameter for itself
            keeps its own value and only takes from the cascade what it left unset, so one input steps out of the
            group without the group having to know. Every parameter is filled in on its own, which is why the
            second input below takes the limits and the formatter from the cascade while overriding the label.
        </div>

        <br />

        <div class="example-box">
            <BitParams Parameters="@fileInputParams">
                <BitFileInput />

                <BitFileInput Label="Its own label, the cascaded rest" MaxSize="1024 * 1024 * 10"
                              MaxSizeErrorMessage="This one alone accepts up to 10 MB." />
            </BitParams>
        </div>

        <br />

        <div class="example-box">
            <BitFileInput Label="Outside the cascade, and back to the defaults" />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Color" RazorCode="@example20RazorCode" Id="example20">
        <div>
            <b>Color</b> picks the theme color of the browse button, its focus ring and the drag-and-drop
            indicator, with <b>Primary</b> as the default. The semantic colors <b>Success</b>, <b>Warning</b>,
            <b>Error</b> and friends tie the input to what the selection means, while the <b>Background</b>,
            <b>Foreground</b> and <b>Border</b> families keep it legible on non-default surfaces like the inverted
            panel below.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.Primary" Label="Primary" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.Secondary" Label="Secondary" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.Tertiary" Label="Tertiary" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.Info" Label="Info" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.Success" Label="Success" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.Warning" Label="Warning" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.SevereWarning" Label="SevereWarning" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.Error" Label="Error" />
            </div>
            <br /><br />
            <div style="background:var(--bit-clr-fg-sec);padding:1rem">
                <div class="example-box">
                    <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.PrimaryBackground" Label="PrimaryBackground" />
                    <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground" Label="SecondaryBackground" />
                    <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.TertiaryBackground" Label="TertiaryBackground" />
                </div>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.PrimaryForeground" Label="PrimaryForeground" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Label="SecondaryForeground" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.TertiaryForeground" Label="TertiaryForeground" />
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-box">
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.PrimaryBorder" Label="PrimaryBorder" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.SecondaryBorder" Label="SecondaryBorder" />
                <BitFileInput Color="BitColor.TertiaryBorder" Label="TertiaryBorder" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="External Icons" RazorCode="@example21RazorCode" Id="example21">
        <div>
            Neither glyph is limited to the built-in icon font: <b>RemoveButtonIcon</b> takes a <b>BitIconInfo</b>
            or a raw CSS class string that can point at any icon library whose stylesheet is loaded, and so does
            whatever a <b>FileIconSelector</b> returns for the file type glyph. Use <b>BitIconInfo.Fa()</b>,
            <b>BitIconInfo.Bi()</b> or <b>BitIconInfo.Css()</b> to build one, or stick with the built-in font and
            just pick a different icon via <b>RemoveButtonIconName</b>.
        </div>

        <br />
        <br />

        <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/7.0.1/css/all.min.css" />

        <div>FontAwesome:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowRemoveButton RemoveButtonIcon="@("fa-solid fa-trash-can")" />
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowRemoveButton
                      RemoveButtonIcon="@BitIconInfo.Css("fa-solid fa-xmark")" />
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton
                      RemoveButtonIcon="@BitIconInfo.Fa("solid trash")"
                      FileIconSelector="@(_ => BitIconInfo.Fa("solid file-lines"))" />

        <br /><br /><br /><br />

        <link rel="stylesheet"
              href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-icons@1.11.3/font/bootstrap-icons.min.css" />

        <div>Bootstrap:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowRemoveButton RemoveButtonIcon="@("bi bi-trash")" />
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowRemoveButton
                      RemoveButtonIcon="@BitIconInfo.Css("bi bi-x-circle-fill")" />
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Label="Browse or drop files" Multiple ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton
                      RemoveButtonIcon="@BitIconInfo.Bi("trash3-fill")"
                      FileIconSelector="@(_ => BitIconInfo.Bi("file-earmark"))" />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Size" RazorCode="@example22RazorCode" Id="example22">
        <div>
            <b>Size</b> scales the browse button, the file list items, their thumbnails and their remove buttons
            together: <b>Small</b> for dense layouts, <b>Medium</b> (the default) for regular forms, and
            <b>Large</b> where the input is a primary control or aimed at touch.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div><b>Small</b>:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Size="BitSize.Small" Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton />
        <br /><br />
        <div><b>Medium</b>:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Size="BitSize.Medium" Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton />
        <br /><br />
        <div><b>Large</b>:</div>
        <br />
        <BitFileInput Size="BitSize.Large" Label="Browse or drop a file" ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton />
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example23RazorCode" Id="example23">
        <div>
            <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the root element. For anything deeper, <b>Styles</b> and
            <b>Classes</b> reach the individual parts - the browse button, the file list, each file item, its
            thumbnail, type glyph, name, folder, size, error message and remove button - and their <b>Dragging</b>
            entry applies to the root only while files are being dragged over the component, which is how the
            examples below restyle the drop state without touching the idle look. Prefer <b>Classes</b> when your
            CSS should own states such as hover and focus, which inline styles cannot express.
        </div>

        <br /><br />

        <div>Component's Style & Class:</div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <BitFileInput Label="Styled file input"
                          Style="box-shadow: dodgerblue 0 0 1rem; border-radius: 1rem; padding: 0.5rem;" />
            <br />
            <BitFileInput Label="Classed file input" Class="custom-class" />
        </div>

        <br /><br /><br />

        <div><b>Styles</b> & <b>Classes</b>:</div>
        <br />
        <div>
            <BitFileInput Label="Styles" ShowRemoveButton Styles="@(new()
                          {
                              Label = "border-color: deeppink; background-color: deeppink; color: white;",
                              Dragging = "background-color: lavenderblush;",
                              FileName = "color: deeppink;",
                              RemoveButton = "background-color: deeppink; color: white;"
                          })" />
            <br />
            <BitFileInput Label="Classes" ShowRemoveButton Classes="@(new()
                          {
                              Label = "custom-label",
                              Dragging = "custom-dragging",
                              FileItem = "custom-item",
                              RemoveButton = "custom-remove"
                          })" />
        </div>

        <br /><br /><br />

        <div>
            The component also reads a set of <BitLink Href="#css-variables-section">CSS variables</BitLink> off its
            root for what no parameter covers. They inherit, so a value on <code>:root</code> or any ancestor
            re-skins every file input below it, and one on the <b>Style</b> of an instance re-skins that one alone.
        </div>
        <br />
        <div>CSS variables:</div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box">
            <BitFileInput Label="Pill button, wide component" ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton
                          Style="--bit-FileInput-label-radius: 999px; --bit-FileInput-max-width: 100%;" />

            <BitFileInput Label="Solid drop indicator in the role color" ShowRemoveButton
                          Style="--bit-FileInput-drop-border-style: solid; --bit-FileInput-drop-background: var(--bit-clr-suc);" />

            <BitFileInput Label="Rounder, roomier items" ShowPreview ShowRemoveButton
                          Style="--bit-FileInput-item-radius: 1rem; --bit-FileInput-item-padding: 1rem; --bit-FileInput-item-gap: 0.75rem; --bit-FileInput-preview-size: 3.5rem;" />

            <BitFileInput Label="A list that scrolls instead of growing" Multiple Append ShowRemoveButton
                          Style="--bit-FileInput-file-list-max-height: 10rem; --bit-FileInput-item-hover-background: var(--bit-clr-bg-sec-hover);" />
        </div>

        <br />

        <div>Set once on an ancestor, inherited by every file input inside it:</div>
        <br />
        <div class="example-box" style="--bit-FileInput-color: rebeccapurple; --bit-FileInput-hover-color: #5b2d8e; --bit-FileInput-item-border-color: rebeccapurple; --bit-FileInput-file-size-color: rebeccapurple;">
            <BitFileInput Label="Attach a résumé" ShowRemoveButton />
            <BitFileInput Label="Attach a cover letter" ShowRemoveButton />
        </div>
    </DemoExample>

    <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example24RazorCode" Id="example24">
        <div>
            Setting <b>Dir</b> to <b>BitDir.Rtl</b> mirrors the whole layout for right-to-left languages - the
            browse button, the thumbnails, the file names, sizes and remove buttons all follow - so the
            BitFileInput reads naturally inside an RTL page like the Farsi example below.
